{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act criminal record screening limits","requirement":"A housing provider may not ask about or consider most criminal records before a conditional offer, publish blanket exclusions, distribute criminal records improperly, or require drug or alcohol tests; after an offer it may consider only specified serious or recent convictions after an individualized assessment.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental dwelling units, excluding owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units; pre-offer inquiry allowed into methamphetamine manufacture on federally assisted housing or lifetime sex-offender registration.","penalty":"Director may impose monetary penalties up to $1,000 for a first violation in 5 years, $5,000 for one prior violation, or $10,000 for two or more prior violations in 7 years.","effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-55","quoted_span":"A housing provider shall not require an applicant to complete any housing application that includes any inquiries regarding an applicant’s criminal record prior to the provision of a conditional offer,","interaction":"Nothing in the act bars independent rights under P.L.1945, c.169 (C.10:5-1 et seq.).","relations":[],"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act application fee disclosure","requirement":"Before accepting any application fee, a housing provider must disclose in writing whether criminal history is part of its eligibility criteria and that the applicant may provide evidence of inaccuracies, rehabilitation, or other mitigating factors; the director may require return of the fee.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental dwelling units, excluding owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":"When a housing provider accepts an application fee.","coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","penalty":"Director may require return of the application fee and impose monetary penalties up to $1,000, $5,000, or $10,000 depending on prior violations.","effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-55","quoted_span":"Prior to accepting any application fee, a housing provider shall disclose in writing to the applicant: (1) Whether the eligibility criteria of the housing provider include the review and consideration of criminal history; and (2) A statement that the applicant, pursuant to subsection a. of this section, may provide evidence demonstrating inaccuracies within the applicant's criminal record or evidence of rehabilitation or other mitigating factors.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act eviction after successful appeal","requirement":"If a housing provider successfully appeals a director's order and the court overturns it, the provider may evict a former applicant who resides in the unit under that order with at least 45 days' notice.","key_value":"45 days' notice","coverage_conditions":"Rental dwelling units, excluding owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units; applies only where a successful appeal overturns a director's order and the former applicant resides under that order.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":"Only after a successful appeal overturns a director's order and the former applicant resides in the unit under that order; at least 45 days' notice required.","coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-63","quoted_span":"then a determination that the housing provider did not violate the provisions of this act as evidenced by such successful appeal shall be grounds for the housing provider to evict the former applicant if that person resides in a rental dwelling unit owned by the housing provider as the result of the director’s order, so long as the housing provider provides the applicant with at least 45 days’ notice prior to the eviction;","interaction":"Notwithstanding the Anti-Eviction Act, a successful appeal is grounds for eviction.","relations":[],"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
